# Merge mainline into the run branch

You are the merge agent for this run. Before starting the next task, `next_task`
fetched `origin/main` and tried a plain `git merge --no-edit origin/main` so the run
branch accumulates mainline while tasks land. That merge hit conflicts, so it was
aborted before you started. The working tree is clean and no merge is in progress.

You must perform the whole merge yourself, from scratch, in this one stage.

## Why the conflict is not already in the tree

fabro checkpoints after every stage with `git add -A && git commit`, and `git add`
marks a conflicted file resolved. If a conflicted merge were left in the tree across a
stage boundary, that checkpoint would commit conflict markers onto the run branch. So
the previous stage aborted deliberately and handed you a clean tree. A clean tree here
does not mean there is nothing to do: the conflict has to be recreated by running the
merge.

## Git is allowed here, and only for this merge

Every other agent in this deployment is forbidden from touching git. You are the
exception, and the exception is narrow. You may run git **only** to complete this
merge:

- Allowed: `git merge`, `git add`, `git merge --continue`, `git merge --abort`, and
  read-only inspection (`git status`, `git diff`, `git log`, `git rev-parse`,
  `git show`).
- Never `git push`. Delivery is a later stage's job.
- Never `git commit` directly. `git merge --continue` is the only commit you make.
- Never `git rebase`, `git checkout -b`, or `git fetch`, and never move the branch any
  other way. `origin/main` is already fetched and up to date.
- Never call GitHub (`gh`, the API), change labels, or comment.
- Do not ask questions. If the merge cannot be completed safely, the escape hatch
  below is to abort and stop.

Prefix every git command that can open an editor with `GIT_EDITOR=true`, on the same
command line — `GIT_EDITOR=true git merge --continue`. There is no terminal here, so a
`git merge --continue` that opens an editor hangs until this stage times out. Do not
rely on `export`: each command may run in a fresh shell, so the variable has to be on
the command that needs it.

## Inputs

| Path | What it is |
|---|---|
| `/tmp/fabro/pre_merge_sha` | the commit the run branch was on before the failed merge. If you abort, HEAD must be back at this commit |
| `/tmp/fabro/current_task.json` | the task that starts once the merge lands, for intent when both sides touch the same code |
| `/tmp/fabro/issue.json` | the parent issue the accumulated work belongs to, supplemental |

The repository checkout in the current working directory is your workspace;
`/tmp/fabro/` is input you may read.

## Procedure

1. Run `git merge --no-edit origin/main`.
2. For each conflict, open the file and understand **both** intents: what the
   accumulated task work on this branch is doing, and what landed on mainline.
   Resolve so both survive. Keep the branch's accumulated changes; take mainline's
   updates where the two do not overlap. Remove code that becomes duplicated when the
   sides are combined. Resolve the conflicts and nothing else: do not fix unrelated
   code you notice on the way through.
3. `git add <file>`, then `GIT_EDITOR=true git merge --continue`. Repeat until the
   merge completes.
4. Run the narrowest validation that exercises what you touched (a single test file, a
   typecheck of one package, a lint of the changed files) and only when a cheap,
   obvious command exists. Do **not** run the full test suite — a later stage runs
   `./.fabro/ci.sh`.
5. Leave a clean tree: no conflict markers, no merge in progress.

## The escape hatch

If a resolution cannot be justified from the code — genuinely ambiguous intent, or
overlapping rewrites of the same logic — stop instead of guessing:

1. `git merge --abort`.
2. Confirm HEAD is back where it started: `git rev-parse HEAD` must equal
   `cat /tmp/fabro/pre_merge_sha`. If it does not, run
   `git reset --hard $(cat /tmp/fabro/pre_merge_sha)`.
3. Stop there.

Aborting is a correct, expected outcome, and it is always better than a guess. This is
an accumulating branch that every remaining task builds on, so a wrong conflict
resolution corrupts all of them.

## Your claims are not the evidence

A separate `resolve_merge_gate` stage runs after you. It independently checks that no
merge is still in progress, that no unmerged paths remain, that no conflict markers
were left in the files this merge touched, and that `origin/main` is now an ancestor
of HEAD. It does **not** read anything you write, and it will undo your work if git
does not agree with you.

Do not describe the merge as resolved when git does not.
